        {
          "source_work_title": "Beyond Capitalism—2",
          "source_locator": "Section 3",
          "quote": "By tracking purchases in real time, it [the Data Matrix] creates a vast statistical mirror of the economy... mapping how individual consumption choices and firms’ production decisions propagate through wider supply chains."
        }
      ],
      "centrality": "core"
    },
    {
      "theme_name": "Total Socialisation: Investment as Conflictual Coordination",
      "overview": "Benanav identifies the 'socialization of investment' as the most transformative element of his model, shifting economic sovereignty from the market (ex-post) to democratic investment bodies (ex-ante). He moves the locus of coordination to 'Investment Boards' and 'Coordinating Committees' which allocate points based on social missions rather than risk-adjusted returns. He rejects 'harmonic planning' (the idea that interests naturally converge) in favor of 'Conflictual Coordination,' which institutionalizes disagreement as the engine of decision-making. Total socialization aims to achieve the 'euthanasia of the rentier' by driving the rate of return on capital to zero through sustained public investment.",
      "key_claims": [
        "Investment is a dynamic, future-making process that determines the conditions for all subsequent production.",
        "Investment Boards should be political assemblies (workers, consumers, community reps), not just technocratic bodies.",
        "Coordinating Committees synchronize cross-sectoral transformations (e.g., Green Transition) with time-limited mandates.",
        "Economic coordination is not about discovering a singular optimal path but navigating trade-offs between competing, valid priorities."
